1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:09,858
JAMIE: Oracle just tanked six point two-five percent, and the company that was supposed to print money off AI just discovered there's a difference between owning the pickaxes and actually finding gold.

2
00:00:09,858 --> 00:00:45,596
ALEX: S&P five hundred down point five-one percent, Nasdaq down one point four-seven, and the V I X just jumped thirteen point nine-three percent—which is the kind of move that usually means someone's unwinding a position they thought was a sure thing. Oracle's down six point two-five percent, A M D's down five point three-three, Broadcom down five point zero-three. The chips are bleeding while Accenture is up five point five-four and Adobe up four point seven-nine. So we have this weird state where the market's selling the names that were supposed to be the AI winners, and it's buying the names that help companies figure out what to do with AI after they buy it.

3
00:00:45,596 --> 00:00:56,378
JAMIE: So the people who sell the shovels are tanking, but the people who teach you how to use the shovels are up. That's—wait, that's actually the opposite of the Oracle thing you opened with.

4
00:00:56,378 --> 00:01:17,636
MAYA: No, it's the same thing, just slower. Oracle thought it could just sit there being the database company and collect rent checks from everyone panicking about AI infrastructure. Accenture is like, 'Hey, we'll come to your office, we'll spend six months in meetings, we'll charge you two million dollars, and we'll tell you that you need Oracle.' It's the same bet, just with more billable hours in between.

5
00:01:17,636 --> 00:01:36,429
ALEX: Money's rotating out of growth and back into bonds. The dollar up point one-two percent. Crude up two point three-four to eighty dollars. Bitcoin down one point five-eight. What you're seeing is the market repricing the entire AI thesis—not killing it, just admitting that the infrastructure play might take longer to monetize than the quarterly earnings deck said it would.

6
00:01:36,429 --> 00:01:42,591
JAMIE: So the V I X spike isn't panic, it's just the sound of a thousand spreadsheets being quietly updated.

7
00:01:42,591 --> 00:01:58,919
JAMIE: The AI super P A C is sitting on thirty-one million dollars, and they're holding it like a loaded gun before the midterms. This is what it looks like when an industry realizes it's about to meet Congress—and Congress still can't agree on whether AI is a toaster or a nuclear reactor.

8
00:01:58,919 --> 00:02:13,091
ALEX: The timing here is the thing. You've got Oracle imploding on earnings, the market repricing AI infrastructure costs upward, and now the industry's pouring money into races where the candidates themselves don't have a coherent position yet. It's defensive spending dressed up as forward positioning.

9
00:02:13,091 --> 00:02:32,192
MAYA: But here's what kills me—the super P A C is spending on races where AI literally isn't the issue. They're not even running ads about AI policy. They're just making sure that whoever wins won't accidentally regulate them while they're still figuring out what their own product does. It's like buying earthquake insurance for a building that hasn't been zoned yet.

10
00:02:32,192 --> 00:02:36,197
JAMIE: So they're not even selling AI to voters. They're just buying silence.

11
00:02:36,197 --> 00:02:50,985
ALEX: Thirty-one million is a lot of silence, but it's also not that much money in a midterm cycle. The real play is that these funds are sitting in accounts right now, which means they're signaling to candidates that more is coming. It's a down payment on access.

12
00:02:50,985 --> 00:03:14,707
MAYA: And the candidates know it. So they're not taking a position on AI regulation because taking a position means you either anger the people writing the checks or you anger the voters who think you're bought. So you just say nothing, pocket the money, and wait for the election to be over. The super P A C gets regulatory uncertainty, which is worth more than a favorable law anyway—it means they keep operating in the gap.

13
00:03:14,707 --> 00:03:33,192
ALEX: SpaceX stock fell below one hundred thirty-five dollars, and the bears are declaring the whole thing a wash. But here's the thing nobody wants to mention: Meta's IPO dropped significantly in the first year and turned into a major company anyway. So the question isn't whether SpaceX is doomed—it's whether the valuation was ever real in the first place.
  ↳ source: https://www.marketwatch.com/story/is-spacexs-stock-a-bust-because-it-fell-below-135-look-what-happened-after-metas-ipo-baed7e68?mod=mw_rss_topstories

14
00:03:33,192 --> 00:03:38,738
JAMIE: So you're saying half the market's reaction to a stock price is just... noise until it isn't?
  ↳ source: https://www.marketwatch.com/story/the-stock-market-has-a-magnificent-seven-problem-but-not-the-one-bears-are-warning-about-574d9dc7?mod=mw_rss_topstories

15
00:03:38,738 --> 00:03:52,909
ALEX: Close to half of major IPOs sink below their offering price and stay there for years. The difference between a disaster and a bargain is usually just how long you're willing to wait. SpaceX could be either one. We just don't get to know yet.

16
00:03:52,909 --> 00:04:02,768
MAYA: Coca-Cola's Fairlife dairy got hit with ransomware and had to shut down production. So the premium milk brand's entire operation just got locked by someone who probably learned coding on YouTube.
  ↳ source: https://techcrunch.com/2026/07/16/coca-cola-suspended-production-at-its-fairlife-dairy-after-a-ransomware-attack/

17
00:04:02,768 --> 00:04:11,394
JAMIE: A teenager in a basement took down a division of one of the world's biggest beverage companies. That's not a cybersecurity problem, that's a business model problem.

18
00:04:11,394 --> 00:04:25,874
MAYA: Fairlife's whole pitch is that it's premium—ultra-filtered, higher protein, better margins. And now it's sitting offline because they probably had a password that was weak with poor security. The ransomware gang doesn't care about the product. They just care that shutting it down costs them money.

19
00:04:25,874 --> 00:04:37,273
ALEX: Chinese fraud rings are stealing billions using coordinated tap-to-pay schemes. They're hitting retailers, banks, the whole chain. This is organized crime operating at scale, and it's showing that cross-border financial controls are still basically Swiss cheese.

20
00:04:37,273 --> 00:04:41,894
JAMIE: That's a significant amount of money. That's not a hack, that's a revenue stream.

21
00:04:41,894 --> 00:04:52,061
ALEX: The infrastructure's been there the whole time. Tap-to-pay exists, international transfers exist, the gaps exist. All that changed is someone decided to run it like a business instead of a one-off score.

22
00:04:52,061 --> 00:04:58,839
MAYA: Politicians are deciding which scientific questions are ideologically acceptable, which is a fantastic way to get bad data and worse policy.

23
00:04:58,839 --> 00:05:03,768
JAMIE: So the government's not just cutting funding—they're curating which questions scientists are allowed to ask.

24
00:05:03,768 --> 00:05:14,551
JAMIE: On the tape tomorrow: Travelers Companies reports earnings on Thursday, and they're walking into a market that just spent the last hour repricing risk. Insurance plays are supposed to be boring until they're not.

25
00:05:14,551 --> 00:05:34,268
ALEX: Travelers is the kind of name that moves on sentiment, not fundamentals. They've got exposure to commercial real estate, to claims inflation, to rate expectations shifting lower. If bonds are rallying like they did today, that's actually good news for their underwriting margins—but the market might just sell them anyway because growth stocks are getting hammered and people need to raise cash somewhere.
  ↳ source: https://www.marketwatch.com/story/the-stock-market-has-a-magnificent-seven-problem-but-not-the-one-bears-are-warning-about-574d9dc7?mod=mw_rss_topstories

26
00:05:34,268 --> 00:05:51,521
MAYA: And then you've got Autoliv on Thursday too—they make safety systems for cars. So we're watching a company that profits when automakers get paranoid about crashes report while the entire chip sector is having an identity crisis. If they miss, it's because the car industry's pulling back. If they beat, it's because paranoia is recession-proof.

27
00:05:51,521 --> 00:05:59,531
JAMIE: So tomorrow's either the sound of the market finding a floor or the sound of it looking for one. Either way, we'll know more by close.

28
00:05:59,531 --> 00:06:06,001
MAYA: Oracle learned today that AI infrastructure isn't a moat—it's a toll booth everyone's suddenly questioning whether they need to use.

29
00:06:06,001 --> 00:06:10,622
ALEX: And by tomorrow, we'll know if anyone else is standing in that same line.

30
00:06:10,622 --> 00:06:14,935
JAMIE: This show is for entertainment and education only — nothing here is investment advice.
